window.__BUNDLE__={"generatedAt":"2026-10-22T17:36:25.299457+00:00","recentDays":7,"archiveDays":1825,"recentCutoff":"2026-10-15","archiveCutoff":"2021-10-23","addedToday":1,"addedThisRun":1,"fetchStats":{"hf":351,"arxiv":1571,"total":1922},"freshness":{"latestPaperDate":"2026-10-22","latestPublishedDate":"2026-10-22","latestBatchCount":298,"newInLast24h":332,"newInLast48h":748},"historyTotal":26666,"topicCounts":{"Multi-Agent":2926,"Evaluation":14565,"Planning":1974,"Memory":527,"Code Agent":1057,"Interpretability":906,"Agentic RAG":1462,"Agentic Search":1942,"RL for LLM":1453,"Reasoning":7249,"Hallucination":1207,"Post-Training":3027,"Alignment":3515,"World Model":526,"Tool Use":1406,"Agent Safety":2643,"Computer Use":358,"Reflection":962,"VLM Training":2070,"LLM Training":3408,"Fine-tuning":4113,"Multimodal Agent":154,"Agent Framework":919,"Error Attribution":171,"Data Engine":530},"count":36,"recentTotal":2649,"archiveCount":0,"archiveTotal":24017,"sources":{"hf":86,"arxiv":2563,"semanticScholar":0},"archiveSources":{"hf":0,"arxiv":0,"semanticScholar":0},"warnings":[],"notes":["archive is split by year under data/archive/ for fast lazy loading; data/history.json keeps the complete database"],"papers":[{"source":"hf","submittedDate":"2026-10-20","authors":["Kuan Zhang","Yukun Chen","Zhihao Yang","Yue Su","Xiangnan Wu","Zirong Chen","Run Luo","Jinkun Hou","Tao Tan","Yinhe Zheng","Yiming Li"],"tags":["LLM Training","Evaluation"],"topics":["LLM Training","Evaluation"],"id":"arxiv:2610.28698","title":"Scaling in Games: Continued Pre-Training For Embodied Agents In Diverse Virtual Worlds","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28698","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28698.pdf","upvotes":46,"announceDate":"2026-10-22","date":"2026-10-22","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.28698","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28698","abstract":"Scaling data, diversity and model capacity has improved embodied learning in physical environments, where diverse experiences remain grounded in shared physical laws. Whether these gains extend to virtual worlds with different visual styles, dynamics, controls, and objectives remains unclear. We investigate this question through a controlled study of continued pre-training (CPT) across three axes: data volume, diversity, and model size. Our 120 training configurations span four model sizes from 0.8B to 27B, six data budgets from 50 to 1,600 hours, and five mixtures ranging from Minecraft-only data to diverse multi-world corpora. We assess performance and generalization using three-level validation losses and gameplay benchmarks from in-corpus worlds to unseen worlds. Our results show: (1) more data improves in-corpus performance, diversity improves transfer to related worlds with limited"},{"source":"hf","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","authors":["Dongfang Li","Zhuoen Chen","Bonian Jia","Zixuan Liu","Zhengxuan Lu","Can Deng","Yukun Shi","Yuhang Meng","Xin Zhang","Jiahe Huang","Fuhao Li","Junmai Wang","Shunyao Wen","Jingjun Sun","Jiaxu Tian","Jiangling Zhang","Zihan Wang","Liangyou Li","Yitong Yao","Shuangyong Song","Beilun Wang","Pengxu Wei","Hanlin Xu","Chang Liu","Yongxiang Li","Baotian Hu","Xiangyang Ji","Min Zhang"],"tags":["Memory","Evaluation"],"topics":["Memory","Evaluation"],"id":"arxiv:2610.29357","title":"Memory-Centric Continual Learning for AI Agents","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29357","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.29357.pdf","upvotes":19,"announceDate":"2026-10-22","date":"2026-10-22","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.29357","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29357","abstract":"Autonomous agents powered by foundation models can reason, plan, and use tools, but their fixed weights and context limits hinder sustained learning from experience over long-term deployment. While external memory systems attempt to bridge this gap, they mostly act as passive retrieval caches, evaluated primarily by whether they can recall stored facts or past interactions. Whether memory can drive genuine continual learning remains an open question. In this paper, we introduce Memory-Centric Continual Learning (MCCL), arguing that memory should be judged by whether it improves future actions, not merely what it retrieves. MCCL has three main parts. First, a three-axis taxonomy organizes memory by where state is stored (substrates), what form it takes (assets), and how it is managed (policies). This defines a two-loop lifecycle that updates memory state in an inner loop while refining ma"},{"source":"hf","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","authors":["Yan Wang"],"tags":["LLM Training","Reasoning","Evaluation"],"topics":["LLM Training","Reasoning","Evaluation"],"id":"arxiv:2610.29680","title":"TraceForge: Distilling with Your Own Thinking","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29680","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.29680.pdf","upvotes":18,"announceDate":"2026-10-22","date":"2026-10-22","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.29680","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29680","abstract":"Using an LLM to generate training data for another is among the most general ways to train one, and it remains common practice. Some frontier teams have closed that route for their own models: what comes back is an answer, or a short summary, without the detailed reasoning trace behind it. That is a little ironic: they were themselves built on distilling human knowledge, and distilling an open-source model is still entirely routine; the restriction applies to their own outputs alone. Recent work recovers the hidden reasoning by replaying encrypted blocks across models. It somehow works, but it breaks the providers' terms of service, so it is not something a product can be built on long-term. We built the alternative. We take the context and the teacher's answer as input, and generate the reasoning trace ourselves, in the style of the student's own model family. Training is two-stage, sup"},{"source":"hf","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","authors":["Yanzhe Chen","Ziyi Yang","Jifeng Zhu","Qiming Huang","Ruihe An","Peiyao Xu","Hesen Yang","Runda Liu","Chang Gong","Zhijun Cao","Zechen Bai","Wenzheng Zeng","Yiqi Lin","Guoqiang Liang","Kevin Yuchen Ma","Kevin Qinghong Lin","Mike Zheng Shou"],"tags":["Reasoning","Evaluation","Multimodal Agent"],"topics":["Reasoning","Evaluation","Multimodal Agent"],"id":"arxiv:2610.30108","title":"Multimodal Embodied Agents: A Unified Capability-centric Perspective from Computer-Use to Robot-Use","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30108","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.30108.pdf","upvotes":17,"announceDate":"2026-10-22","date":"2026-10-22","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.30108","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30108","abstract":"Multimodal agents (MMAs) sustain interaction through reasoning, memory, tools, and feedback, most visibly as computer-use agents, while robotic systems (RSs) couple sensing and actuation under physical dynamics. We define multimodal embodied agents (MMEAs) as goal-directed systems that couple multimodal task reasoning with physical action and revise decisions from the resulting feedback. This raises a central question: what changes when a multimodal agent moves from computer-use to robot-use? We introduce PAPAV, a unified capability-centric framework of five recurring functions: Perceive the current state, Anticipate action effects, Plan a feasible course, Act through an interface or body, and Verify the outcome. Defined by function rather than architecture, these capabilities let PAPAV identify what all three share and then compare MMEAs with MMAs and with RSs. Five physical constraints"},{"source":"hf","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","authors":["Yanqing Liu","Letian Zhang","Chengzhi Liu","Zijun Wang","Jieru Mei","Hongliang Fei","Yuyin Zhou","Cihang Xie"],"tags":["LLM Training","Planning","Evaluation","Alignment","World Model"],"topics":["LLM Training","Planning","Evaluation","Alignment","World Model"],"id":"arxiv:2610.30120","title":"ALPS: Visual State Encoders for World Models","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30120","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.30120.pdf","upvotes":16,"announceDate":"2026-10-22","date":"2026-10-22","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.30120","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30120","abstract":"World models turn visual representations into predictions and actions. We argue that their visual foundation should combine physical-state information, native language alignment, and pixel reconstruction, so that a single representation can support prediction, instruction, and inspection. However, existing encoders typically specialize in only one or two of these capabilities; we therefore introduce ALPS, a pretrained visual state encoder that unifies all three in a single set of patch tokens. Using only still images and text, we pretrain a ViT-B/16 from scratch with language, reconstruction, and spatial correspondence objectives, and then refine it via cross-view self-distillation. In a unified evaluation across fifteen public encoders, ALPS achieves the lowest aggregate LIBERO state-probe error among ViT-B models (matching the 3.5x larger V-JEPA 2-L) while maintaining strong language a"},{"source":"hf","submittedDate":"2026-10-20","authors":["Yujian Liu","Li An","Zelin He","Jacob Helwig","Jiabao Ji","Rohit Jain","Siyu Zhu","Shiyu Chang"],"tags":["Reasoning"],"topics":["Reasoning"],"id":"arxiv:2610.28887","title":"Building AI Research Teams for Autonomous Discovery of Linear Attention Architectures","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28887","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28887.pdf","upvotes":15,"announceDate":"2026-10-22","date":"2026-10-22","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.28887","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28887","abstract":"Autonomous agents can increasingly propose ideas, modify code, and run experiments, creating an opportunity to accelerate scientific discovery using teams of collaborative AI researchers. To escape local minima in the discovery, we propose to encourage orthogonal exploration by constructing the team using agents with varying expertise. However, realizing this design requires more than diverse backgrounds. When experiments are costly, a team can test only a limited number of ideas, thereby creating the need to balance exploration and exploitation when selecting a subset of researchers to propose and execute new experiments. Furthermore, each result should shape what the team tries next. We therefore ask how AI researchers with different scientific backgrounds can build on one another's findings and sustain progress under a limited experimental budget. To address these challenges, we build"},{"source":"hf","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","authors":["Fei Bai","Huatong Song","Chuan Hao","Ran Tao","Jian Yang","Bryan Dai","Wayne Xin Zhao","Ji-Rong Wen"],"tags":["Evaluation"],"topics":["Evaluation"],"id":"arxiv:2610.29458","title":"Harness Scaling for Generalizable Agentic Reinforcement Learning","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29458","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.29458.pdf","upvotes":14,"announceDate":"2026-10-22","date":"2026-10-22","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.29458","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29458","abstract":"Agent harnesses have become a central component of modern agentic systems, shaping how models use tools, manage context, and coordinate long-horizon interaction. Realizing the benefits of these harnesses depends on how effectively the underlying model can make use of them, motivating reinforcement learning directly through the harness. However, existing approaches typically train through a single fixed harness, which can lead to specialization and limited transfer across harness configurations. To address this, we introduce \\textbf{Harness Scaling}, a new scaling dimension for agentic RL that extends training from a single harness to diverse execution configurations. Concretely, we construct a factorized harness space, select a compact training subset with balanced factorial coverage, and organize cross-harness training to combine global coverage with controlled variation for the same ta"},{"source":"hf","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","authors":["Yifan Zhou","Ziyang Gong","Xiaojun Ye","Yifei Liu","Donggang Li","Xiaolong Zhou","Zhihang Zhong","Xue Yang"],"tags":["Reasoning","Evaluation"],"topics":["Reasoning","Evaluation"],"id":"arxiv:2610.29317","title":"SpatialBrain: Spatial Intelligence Growing from Experience","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29317","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.29317.pdf","upvotes":12,"announceDate":"2026-10-22","date":"2026-10-22","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.29317","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29317","abstract":"Spatial reasoning requires more than access to geometric tools: a reasoner must decide when direct observation is sufficient and when to reconstruct, measure, or change reference frames. We present SpatialBrain, a framework that retains experience about these decisions while keeping its vision-language backbone fixed. The key distinction is between evidence about a scene and experience about how to reason with that evidence. 3DFusionMemory fuses current-scene observations into queryable spatial relations. A Spatial Skill Library provides reusable tool procedures, and Spatial Cognition associates task conditions with experience about when and how to use memory and skills. Completed trajectories are abstracted into process records and consolidated between task blocks, allowing later decisions to draw on earlier execution without transferring scene coordinates or reference answers. Across f"},{"source":"hf","submittedDate":"2026-10-20","authors":["Chuanmeng Bian","Daren Chen","Peixin Chen","Zhigao Chen","Chong Deng","Zhiyun Fan","Zhifu Gao","Bo Gong","Qing Gu","Jiajun He","Yawei Hu","Yunjie Ji","Wei Ju","Jingbei Li","Xiangang Li","Xu Li","Zengxi Li","Zheng Li","Chengdong Liang","Baiji Liu","Yaming Liu","Ying Liu","Bin Ma","Yiping Peng","Yuezhang Peng","Zhendong Peng","Yu Pu","Yang Shi","Xin Shu","Jian Tang","Biao Tian","Peiyao Wang","Tianzi Wang","Wen Wang","Wupeng Wang","Cheng Wen","Yuzhong Wu","Zijian Xia","Yunchong Xiao","Nan Yang","Jianwei Yu","Jixing Yu","Binbin Zhang","Lei Zhang","Sitong Zhao","Guangdong Zhou","Yuan Zhou","Jianheng Zhuo"],"tags":["Reasoning"],"topics":["Reasoning"],"id":"arxiv:2610.28711","title":"Qwen-Audio-3.1-ASR Technical Report","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28711","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28711.pdf","upvotes":12,"announceDate":"2026-10-22","date":"2026-10-22","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.28711","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28711","abstract":"We present Qwen-Audio-3.1-ASR, a family of audio-language models that builds upon its predecesor Qwen-Audio-3.0-ASR with improved speech recognition and expanded capabilities in speaker-diarized transcription and general audio understanding. The family retains support for 30 languages and 16 Chinese dialects, together with instruction-controlled capabilities including historical-context conditioning, hierarchical hotword customization, and native transcription polishing. Speaker-diarized ASR produces structured outputs containing speaker labels, timestamps, and recognized text, maintaining consistent speaker labels across turns and the representation of overlapping speech. The general audio understanding capabilities cover audio-, speech-, and music-captioning, temporal grounding and sound event detection, as well as audio question answering and multiple-choice reasoning. These tasks bro"},{"source":"hf","submittedDate":"2026-10-20","authors":["Ming Zhong","Stacy Li","Nicholas Carlini","Matthew Jagielski"],"tags":["Code Agent","Evaluation"],"topics":["Code Agent","Evaluation"],"id":"arxiv:2610.28763","title":"Automating ML for Science: Can Frontier Agents Climb Scientific Hills in the Wild?","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28763","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28763.pdf","upvotes":11,"announceDate":"2026-10-22","date":"2026-10-22","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.28763","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28763","abstract":"Frontier coding agents are increasingly framed as end-to-end automated researchers, and ML for science offers a demanding test of that promise: improving a scientific model often requires domain insight beyond routine ML engineering. We introduce Agent4Sci, a long-horizon testbed for measuring how far today's agents can improve the published best models on scientific ML benchmarks, spanning 20 benchmarks across 11 domains. Rather than ranking agents on a leaderboard, Agent4Sci asks what kinds of progress agents can make on these tasks under in-the-wild conditions, and where they fall short. Empirically, today's frontier agents advance the published best on every benchmark, with Claude Opus reaching 10.4% mean relative improvement and no run flagged for academic misconduct. These gains, however, come almost entirely from a bag of ML-engineering tricks rather than research-level insight. T"},{"source":"hf","authors":["Zhuoyang Liu","Dantong Niu","Botong Zhang","Boning Shao","Jing Wang","Ruijie Zheng","Yuvan Sharma","Runyu Lu","Jiaming Liu","Renrui Zhang","Roei Herzig","Yutong Bai","Ken Goldberg","Danfei Xu","Yuke Zhu","Guanzhi Wang","Linxi \"Jim\" Fan","Trevor Darrell"],"tags":["Agent Safety","Reasoning","Planning","Evaluation","World Model"],"topics":["Agent Safety","Reasoning","Planning","Evaluation","World Model"],"id":"arxiv:2610.30136","title":"Agentic World Action Models","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30136","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.30136.pdf","upvotes":10,"date":"2026-10-22","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.30136","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30136","abstract":"Recent world action models provide visual foresight and future prediction for robot manipulation, but lack explicit mechanisms for compositional skill reuse. Agentic robotics combines code-based planning and reasoning with reusable skills, but often lacks predictive grounding in physical dynamics. We introduce AW-0, an agentic world action model framework that integrates code-based planning with joint video and action prediction. We jointly model code, future video, and robot actions as three coupled generative streams within a unified mixture-of-transformers architecture. Within this architecture a code expert generates parameterized skill routines and control logic while video and action experts generate future visual states and low-level motor commands. Code serves two complementary roles: it structures multi-step prediction and control within the world model and represents skills as "},{"source":"hf","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","authors":["My Chiffon Nguyen","Tianyi Hu","Vinod Anbalagan","Henry Gagnier","Pouya Sadeghi","Farah Abdou","Tidor-Vlad Pricope","Diego MMS Oliveira","Jocelyn K D'Arcy","Madeline Smith","Oluwaseun A Ajayi","Yusser Al Ghussin","Zena Al-Khalili","Micol Altomare","Shayan Amir","Ahanaf Aziz","Bryan Chen","Ruchira Dhar","Abdellah El Mekki","Rawan Mohammed Elframawy","Akhil Eppa","Silvia Fernandez","Alex Gimei","Arya Hariharan","Christian Hoang","RamMohanRao Kadiyala","Diana Kozachek","Peerat Limkonchotiwat","Ankita Maity","Di Mo","Ayush Munot","Aylin Naebzadeh","Anastasiia Nikitina","Dina Pisarevskaya","Divas Poudel","Jebish Purbey","Md Rabius Sany Apu","Ahmad Mufli Ramadhan","Ralitza Nikolova Rizova","Diogo M. Serrano-Miranda","Vivek Gopalkrishna Silimkhan","Luis Fernando Solis Navarro","Kaiser Sun","Arya Suneesh","Manaswitha Sunkara","Tejaswi Sunkara","Jonathan E Swindell","Hanna Yukhymenko","Jingyi Zheng","Esther Adenuga","Ilia Afanasev","Wafa Aissa","Lisa Alazraki","Lauren C Altomare","Priscilla Amondi Amuok","Emile Anand","Gabriel Assis","José Matías Avalos","Silvia Lorena Avila","Zakaria Baannou","Niyati Bafna","Yash Bagla","Sarah Bahrami","Bontu F Balcha","Vanya Bannihatti Kumar","Irene Baucells","Abdumutalib Begmuratov","Naman Bhatia","Souvik Bhattacharyya","Isabel Blanco Ferreiro","Felipe Bochini","Sayuru Bopitiya","Angana Borah","Tulika Bose","Olena Bugaiova","Juliana Carvalho","Nancy F Chen","Stephy Chen","Rochelle Choenni","Jayanth Krishna Chundru","Leandro A Cossio","Nebojša Damnjanović","Ona de Gibert","Marco De Santis","Pedro Delgado","Kaustubh Dholé","Adnan El Assadi","Wesam El-Sayed","Nadine M Elframawy","Oumayma Essarhi","Naome A Etori","Reuben Chagas Fernandes","Saanidhi Pravin Gade","Javier García Gilabert","Álvaro González-Escapa","Bin Hu","Kaiyu Huang","Abderrahmane Issam","Danka Jokić","Ulugbek Khalilov","Dominik Krzemiński","Roy Ka-Wei Lee","Haichang Li","Chen Cecilia Liu","Zhengyuan Liu","Cedric Lothritz","Houman Mehrafarin","Antonio Miranda-Escalada","Fengran Mo","Mehrnaz Mofakhami","Wafaa Mohammed","Felipe Moreno-Vera","Prahitha Movva","Jannatul Nayem","Van Ngo","Joy Olusanya","Marc Pàmies","Avinash Kuney Pandey","Yurii Paniv","Fred Philippy","Vladislav Poritski","Dikshya Poudel","Joanna Radoła","Sara Rajaee","Swati Rajwal","Prerana Rane","Muhammad Ravi Shulthan Habibi","Lucas Resck","Shubhashis Roy Dipta","Ananya Sahu","Eduardo Sánchez","Borja Sánchez Clemente","Aleix Sant","Nobin Sarwar","Istiak Shihab","Santhoshini Pranathi Singaraju","Jane Smith","David Stap","Emily Z Su","Ananth K Suresh","Sajag Swami","Ruiyang Tan","Nazia Tasnim","Khalid Tounoussi","Patteera Triamamornwooth","Muhammad Saad Umar","Shankar Venkitachalam","Oksana Volchek","Lingbo Wang","Debela Desalegn Yadeta","Rushikesh Zawar","Jinghan Zhang","Mike Zhang","Weihua Zheng","Marzieh Fadaee","Akhil Arora","Károly Boczka","Julia Kreutzer"],"tags":["Reasoning","Evaluation"],"topics":["Reasoning","Evaluation"],"id":"arxiv:2610.29968","title":"CulturalRiddles: A Multicultural Benchmark of Riddles","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29968","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.29968.pdf","upvotes":10,"announceDate":"2026-10-22","date":"2026-10-22","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.29968","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29968","abstract":"As LLMs expand their language coverage, evaluating how well they understand and represent diverse cultures becomes increasingly important. However, many benchmarks remain English-centric, with limited cultural coverage and a focus on factual knowledge over its application in real-world cultural contexts. We propose CulturalRiddles, a benchmark of original, culturally grounded riddles created entirely by humans through a participatory community effort, spanning 62 cultures (45 countries, 51 languages). These riddles are deeply rooted in their respective cultures and require abductive and compositional reasoning, alongside detailed factual and social knowledge, language skills, and an understanding of culturally specific associations. We benchmark 28 LLM configurations using both automatic and large-scale human evaluation. Even frontier models achieve at most 22% accuracy in some communiti"},{"source":"hf","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","authors":["Zhipeng Chen","Yunsen Liang","Yanxi Chen","Hongkang Zhao","Lingyun Feng","Ruiyang Ren","Guoxin Chen","Jia Deng","Feng Chang","Chuan Hao","Ran Tao","Jian Yang","Ji-Rong Wen","Bryan Dai","Wayne Xin Zhao"],"tags":["Reasoning","Evaluation","Reflection"],"topics":["Reasoning","Evaluation","Reflection"],"id":"arxiv:2610.29784","title":"Evolving with Open Worlds: Towards Recursive Self-Improvement for Future Forecasting","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29784","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.29784.pdf","upvotes":9,"announceDate":"2026-10-22","date":"2026-10-22","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.29784","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29784","abstract":"Most contemporary artificial intelligence (AI) systems are released with frozen parameters and improve through external updates, whereas an ideal system would keep improving from its own experience, a capacity we call recursive self-improvement (RSI). Yet studying RSI requires an environment that is continuously changing, objectively verifiable, and naturally grounded, posing a fundamental challenge for agent evolution. Here we introduce DeepEvolve, a sustainable evolution agent system grounded in future event prediction, in which agents continuously interact with a dynamically evolving environment and improve by internalizing and externalizing the experience gained through these interactions. A living environment synthesizes verifiable tasks, and a recursive-evolution engine turns the reasoning trajectory behind each prediction back into capability: decoupling search from prediction ada"},{"source":"hf","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","authors":["Juan S. Santillana"],"tags":["Evaluation"],"topics":["Evaluation"],"id":"arxiv:2610.30090","title":"VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter Triage - An empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30090","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.30090.pdf","upvotes":7,"announceDate":"2026-10-22","date":"2026-10-22","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.30090","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30090","abstract":"Perimeter triage faces a latency/semantics trade-off: signature engines are fast and format-safe but blind to unseen attacks, while generative models are semantic but slow and produce unparseable output. In late 2026 a class of commercial System One decision models (TypeSafe Jev; Microsoft-Decision-1) began selling typed decision primitives - binary gates, closed-set choices, ordinal scores - as product APIs, with no academic treatment. We present VX-One: a small multi-head network (100K-10M parameters) whose outputs are these primitives - a calibrated gate, a choice over a closed candidate set that always includes an explicit unknown option, and an ordinal score, trained multi-task, temperature-calibrated per head (~0.7-1.7 ms p50 on CPU). We evaluate it on UNSW-NB15, CIC-IDS2017, EMBER2024 against calibrated XGBoost, Random-Forest, MLP, Kitsune, and measure a two-phase cascade (Suricat"},{"source":"hf","authors":["Haocheng Wang","Youquan Liu","Weiyu Guo","Aokai Zhang","Donghang Wu","Hongyu Liu","Sha Zhang","Weidong Yang","Ben Fei"],"tags":["Evaluation"],"topics":["Evaluation"],"id":"arxiv:2610.27861","title":"RoboCasa-Shift: Stage-Aware Robustness Evaluation of Vision-Language-Action Policies in Long-Horizon Household Manipulation","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27861","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.27861.pdf","upvotes":2,"date":"2026-10-22","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.27861","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27861","abstract":"Vision-language-action (VLA) robustness is commonly summarized by aggregate task success, which obscures how individual executions change under distribution shifts and where failures emerge in long-horizon tasks. We introduce RoboCasa-Shift, a diagnostic robustness benchmark built on RoboCasa365 that combines controlled single-factor shifts, matched clean-perturbed rollouts in which each pair shares the same underlying settings and differs only in the target shift, and simulator-grounded stage-aware diagnostics. We evaluate GR00T N1.5 on four long-horizon tasks that pass a predefined clean-performance gate under four perturbation factors and three calibrated severity levels, including conditions that drive the evaluated policy to complete task failure, totaling 1,040 formal rollouts. Robustness responses vary substantially across factors and tasks, and increasing severity does not always"},{"source":"arxiv","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","authors":["Ido Levy","Segev Shlomov","Leshem Choshen"],"tags":["Agentic RAG","Agentic Search","Evaluation"],"topics":["Agentic RAG","Agentic Search","Evaluation"],"id":"arxiv:2610.29722","title":"Retrieval Augmented Generation Compositionality","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29722","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.29722.pdf","upvotes":22,"announceDate":"2026-10-22","date":"2026-10-22","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.29722","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29722","abstract":"Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) grounds large language models in external knowledge. Modern pipelines combine embedding, chunking, retrieval, reranking, query augmentation, context compression, and agentic verification, yet these techniques are typically evaluated one at a time. It remains unclear whether their isolated gains survive composition, whether retrieval quality and answer faithfulness favor the same design choices, and whether conclusions from small corpora hold at large scale. We pre-declare three hypotheses and introduce LASAGNA, an open-source framework for controlled RAG evaluation. We test 450 configurations across eight benchmarks and corpora ranging from 5K to 200K documents. Three findings emerge. First, individually strong techniques from different phases of a RAG pipeline do not necessarily compose: three retrieval techniques predict a +47% combined improvement "},{"source":"arxiv","submittedDate":"2026-10-19","authors":["Jaesang Yu","Seoyeon Byun","Yun Wang","Yan Lu","Juho Kim"],"tags":["Reasoning","Evaluation"],"topics":["Reasoning","Evaluation"],"id":"arxiv:2610.26542","title":"Breaking Diffusion: Parallel Reasoning with Draft Refinement","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.26542","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.26542.pdf","upvotes":20,"announceDate":"2026-10-22","date":"2026-10-22","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.26542","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.26542","abstract":"Drafting an answer allows a reasoning model to reconsider each decision in the context of the others. Yet repeated diffusion denoising does not ensure that individually plausible token updates form a better answer together. We introduce Draft Refinement Language Models (DRLMs), which use a single denoiser to propose parallel revisions and evaluate a candidate draft. We train the denoiser to reconstruct every token from uniformly corrupted drafts, teaching both preservation and repair. Its predictions specify revisions directly, without a diffusion reverse transition. Evaluation requires a different use of these predictions: confidence in an existing token reflects both contextual support and evidence from observing the token itself. Using the known corruption process, we correct for this self-token evidence to estimate how well each token is supported by the prompt and the remaining draf"},{"source":"arxiv","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","authors":["Chenxu Zhou","Zhiyuan Yu","Yudong Jin","Yinji Shentu","Yu Zhang","Jiaming Sun","Xiaowei Zhou"],"tags":["Evaluation"],"topics":["Evaluation"],"id":"arxiv:2610.30023","title":"RealMe: Immersive Volumetric Video from Minimal Captures","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30023","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.30023.pdf","upvotes":12,"announceDate":"2026-10-22","date":"2026-10-22","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.30023","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30023","abstract":"This paper addresses the problem of creating immersive volumetric videos from sparse and low-resolution capture resources. Conventional dense multi-view reconstruction methods can produce high-quality volumetric videos, but their reliance on costly capture setups makes them impractical in real-world scenarios. Recent approaches use generative models to relax capture requirements, but still suffer from limited resolution and 4D inconsistency. To overcome these constraints, we propose RealMe, a unified framework built around a high-resolution video diffusion model that enhances limited captures into high-resolution (2K), 4D-consistent videos for volumetric video reconstruction. Specifically, RealMe constructs auxiliary data from capture videos and encodes them into latents along with layout-aware positional embeddings. The video diffusion model adapts factorized spatio-temporal attention t"},{"source":"arxiv","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","authors":["Zhanshuo Cao","Jiakai Tang","Yuqi Zhou","Sunhao Dai","Jun Xu"],"tags":["Reasoning","Evaluation"],"topics":["Reasoning","Evaluation"],"id":"arxiv:2610.30128","title":"Think Harder Where It Matters: Item-Level Adaptive Recursion for Sequential Recommendation","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30128","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.30128.pdf","upvotes":10,"announceDate":"2026-10-22","date":"2026-10-22","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.30128","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30128","abstract":"Existing sequential recommenders usually allocate a fixed computational budget to every item in a user's interaction history, regardless of the item's contribution to the final prediction. We challenge this de facto design with two diagnostic findings. (1) Non-uniform computational need: items within the same sequence vary drastically in how much computation they require. Most converge after shallow processing while a critical minority only stabilizes at full depth. (2) Suboptimality of fixed allocation: given oracle access to the target item, a non-uniform depth assignment consistently outranks the fixed one, indicating that existing uniform computation both wastes capacity on already-converged items and sacrifices overall recommendation quality. These observations motivate a shift from fixed-depth computation to item-level adaptive reasoning. To this end, we propose LoopRec. LoopRec re"},{"source":"arxiv","submittedDate":"2026-10-20","authors":["Xiaoxiao Ma","Weiyang Jin","Yanwen Ma","Haoran Li","Zhixiang Wei","Songchun Zhang","Yaowei Li","Junhao Zhuang","Zeyue Xue","Yuming Li","Zheming Fu","Haoyang Huang","Nan Duan","Feng Zhao"],"tags":["Planning","World Model"],"topics":["Planning","World Model"],"id":"arxiv:2610.28827","title":"WorldStream: Streaming Interactive 3D Worlds with Hierarchical Planning and Persistent State","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28827","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28827.pdf","upvotes":9,"announceDate":"2026-10-22","date":"2026-10-22","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.28827","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28827","abstract":"We present WorldStream, an open-ended 3D world model with three simultaneous capabilities: it extends playable terrain beyond any pre-specified map, acts on open-ended language in the running world, and restores visited places with their edits. A hierarchical planner over a typed decision model emits structured decision state: Macro fixes settlement, ecology, transport, and hydrological structure across a group of tiles; Micro specializes that contract into tile semantics under measured terrain and accepted neighbor commitments. Deterministic local code compiles accepted plans into terrain, roads, settlements, vegetation, and entities. Streaming separates their reach: plans run ahead along the predicted corridor, compilation only where movement will soon need it, geometry behind the user released. One world record retains plans, seeds, object identities and mutable state, boundary commit"},{"source":"arxiv","submittedDate":"2026-10-20","authors":["Alan-Barsag Gazzaev","Alexey Gavrilov","Mikhail Mozikov","Sergey Muravyov"],"tags":["Multi-Agent","Planning","Evaluation"],"topics":["Multi-Agent","Planning","Evaluation"],"id":"arxiv:2610.28909","title":"DiffResearch: Accelerating Deep Research with Diffusion Language Models","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28909","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28909.pdf","upvotes":4,"announceDate":"2026-10-22","date":"2026-10-22","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.28909","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28909","abstract":"Deep research systems iteratively plan, gather evidence, synthesize, and revise, making end-to-end latency a key bottleneck for interactive use. Almost all production systems are built on autoregressive (AR) language models and inherit left-to-right decoding in planning and long-form writing. We present DiffResearch, an open multi-agent deep research framework that places a diffusion language model (dLLM) at the core of every stage, and measure how a diffusion backbone changes latency and report quality. Holding the agentic scaffold, retrieval code, prompts, and evaluation fixed, we swap the backbone between an open diffusion model and a comparable AR model. Across 100 Deep Research Bench tasks, the diffusion backbone is roughly 6 times faster end-to-end, driven by order-of-magnitude reductions in planning and writing. We further introduce dFast, a decomposition mode that reads intermedi"},{"source":"arxiv","submittedDate":"2026-10-20","authors":["Alan-Barsag Gazzaev","Alexey Gavrilov","Sergey Muravyov","Ilya Makarov"],"tags":["Multi-Agent"],"topics":["Multi-Agent"],"id":"arxiv:2610.28905","title":"Hidden Messages Between Diffusion Language Model Agents: Causal Evidence for Latent Communication","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28905","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28905.pdf","upvotes":4,"announceDate":"2026-10-22","date":"2026-10-22","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.28905","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28905","abstract":"Diffusion language models form answers through a sequence of revisable continuous states, but multi-agent systems usually discard those states and make agents communicate only after decoding text. We ask whether one independent diffusion trajectory can pass a compact hidden message into another and, crucially, whether the receiver uses what that message means. In Hidden Messages, a sender compresses its denoising state into four signed dense vectors; the receiver consumes them while retaining its own context, canvas, and trajectory. Removing a message tests whether the channel matters; replacing it with a message from another example tests whether its content matters. Across controlled tasks, derangement removes a +63.6-point advantage on Tiny-A2D and a +100.0-point advantage on Dream-7B, while targeted substitutions steer the receiver toward the substituted fact. On QASC with the two su"},{"source":"arxiv","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","authors":["Houde Qian","Zheng Jiang","Wenyi Li","Chenxi Xu","Xinjie Lin","Yuzhao Peng","Ruoyu Han","Youjie Zheng","Kaisen Yang","Qinhuai Na","You Fu","Calvin Xiao"],"tags":["Evaluation"],"topics":["Evaluation"],"id":"arxiv:2610.30113","title":"Quant-Arena: Benchmarking LLM Agents for Quantitative Trading Research","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30113","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.30113.pdf","upvotes":3,"announceDate":"2026-10-22","date":"2026-10-22","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.30113","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30113","abstract":"Quantitative research is an iterative loop: a researcher proposes a market hypothesis, implements it as an executable signal, tests it on historical data, and decides what to keep. Existing benchmarks score formulas, code, or individual trading decisions, leaving open whether large language models (LLMs) can complete this loop and produce strategies that work beyond the research period. We introduce Quant-Arena, a benchmark and public leaderboard for end-to-end quantitative research on real A-share market data. A model constructs features and stock-scoring code, receives feedback only on 2022--2023, and freezes one strategy for assessment on the sealed year 2024. A cash-account evaluator applies trading constraints and costs, including market impact estimated from a simulated order book. Scores are dominated by realized excess return and complemented by signal quality and the diversity o"},{"source":"arxiv","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","authors":["Saiteja Utpala","Chieh-Hsin Lai","Yuki Mitsufuji","Praneeth Vepakomma"],"tags":["Reasoning"],"topics":["Reasoning"],"id":"arxiv:2610.30066","title":"Looped Uniform Diffusion Language Models","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30066","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.30066.pdf","upvotes":2,"announceDate":"2026-10-22","date":"2026-10-22","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.30066","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30066","abstract":"Uniform diffusion language models (UDLMs) generate text by iteratively denoising sequences of ordinary vocabulary tokens, any of which may be corrupted and subsequently revised. During training, UDLMs must denoise from a context that may itself be unreliable, whereas masked diffusion language models (MDLMs) always condition on a clean context. This challenging training objective makes UDLMs more FLOP-intensive to train than MDLMs, but it yields stronger downstream reasoning performance. Recently, selective looping, which recursively applies a few early Transformer layers within each denoising step, has been shown to improve training efficiency in MDLMs under an iso-FLOP budget. We investigate looping in UDLMs while keeping the training objective and sampler unchanged. We find that looping significantly improves training efficiency: looped UDLMs reach the validation NLL of a non-looped UD"},{"source":"arxiv","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","authors":["Nithin Sivakumaran","Ali Payani","Hyunji Lee","Mohit Bansal","Elias Stengel-Eskin"],"tags":["Multi-Agent","Reasoning"],"topics":["Multi-Agent","Reasoning"],"id":"arxiv:2610.29934","title":"ToM2Vec: Continuous Theory-of-Mind Tokens for Multi-Agent Coordination","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29934","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.29934.pdf","upvotes":1,"announceDate":"2026-10-22","date":"2026-10-22","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.29934","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29934","abstract":"Forming an accurate theory-of-mind (ToM) is key to successful multi-agent collaboration and requires reasoning under uncertainty to capture multiple possible partner states. This is hard to do in the text representation space native to large language model (LLM) agents, which more easily describe a single partner state. To address this challenge, we introduce ToM2Vec, which learns to form abstract theories-of-mind in a rich latent space via a fixed budget of continuous reasoning tokens. During training, supervision for these tokens comes from the partner's actual thoughts, which the ToM tokens are trained to abstract and match. At test-time, ToM tokens are generated by each agent and act as conditioners for that agent's thoughts and actions. Across several environments covering collaborative, competitive, and mixed-motive settings (Overcooked-AI, multiparty negotiation, Decrypto, Hanabi,"},{"source":"arxiv","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","authors":["Jiongxiao Wang","Han Ding","Youzhi Luo","Yawei Wang","Yun Zhou","Linbo Liu","Panpan Xu"],"tags":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Reasoning","Fine-tuning","Data Engine"],"topics":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Reasoning","Fine-tuning","Data Engine","World Model"],"id":"arxiv:2610.29139","title":"Steering Black-Box Large Language Model Agents with Pitfall Anticipation World Model","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29139","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.29139.pdf","upvotes":0,"announceDate":"2026-10-22","date":"2026-10-22","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29139","abstract":"Frontier black-box Large Language Model (LLM) agents are proficient at long-horizon reasoning and precise action generation. However, when it comes to a specific agentic task, they have no internalized knowledge of how that particular environment unfolds. A world model offers a natural remedy, providing the agent with environment specific knowledge by foreseeing how the environment will respond to the action it is about to take. Prior world models for LLM agents mainly predict next observations at each step, which is costly and often uninformative because individual observations provide limited task-relevant guidance and not every step requires call for foresight. This raises a key question: what a world model should predict and when should it be invoked for agentic tasks? Our pilot study suggests that world model guidance is most useful when it anticipates pitfalls within a bounded mult"},{"source":"arxiv","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","authors":["Qingqing Wang","Liqiang Xiao","Chang Chang"],"tags":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Multi-Agent","Reasoning","Planning"],"topics":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Multi-Agent","Reasoning","Planning","Evaluation"],"id":"arxiv:2610.29010","title":"An Evidence-Driven Multi-Agent Compound AI System for Head and Neck Proton Radiotherapy Planning","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29010","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.29010.pdf","upvotes":0,"announceDate":"2026-10-22","date":"2026-10-22","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29010","abstract":"Radiation treatment planning requires balancing competing objectives to satisfy clinical criteria while accommodating physicians' preferences. This study aims to develop an evidence-driven, multi-agent compound AI system that integrates DRL-based automated treatment planning with LLM-based conversational plan refinement for proton PBS therapy. A policy network trained using PPO first generates multiple plans without human intervention, from which one is selected as the target plan and the others serve as references for subsequent refinements. For challenging cases requiring further adjustments, an LLM-based multi-agent system enables plan refinement through natural language interactions. The system comprises a planning agent that recommends evidence-supported adjustments, a chatbot providing a unified conversational interface, and an evaluation agent that retrieves clinical metrics and i"},{"source":"arxiv","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","authors":["Biswas Poudel","Nilson Chapagain","Amit Kumar","Xianshun Jiang"],"tags":["Post-Training","Reasoning","Planning","Hallucination","Evaluation"],"topics":["Post-Training","Reasoning","Planning","Hallucination","Evaluation","RL for LLM"],"id":"arxiv:2610.29189","title":"Reasoning-Search-Augmented LLMs: An Operational Taxonomy, Benchmark Analysis and Controlled Evidence Synthesis","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29189","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.29189.pdf","upvotes":0,"announceDate":"2026-10-22","date":"2026-10-22","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29189","abstract":"Large language models (LLMs) demonstrate impressive emergent reasoning capabilities, yet they remain constrained by fixed model parameters and finite context windows. Even when augmented with static retrieval, they can produce statements that are unsupported by, or even contradictory to, retrieved evidence. This leads to hallucinations, difficulty incorporating up-to-date information, and failures to correctly interpret or reconcile sources. Recent work addresses these limitations by tightly coupling step-by-step reasoning with interactive search, treating retrieval as an action within an iterative agentic loop rather than a one-shot preprocessing step. This study reviews frameworks that implement such reasoning-search loops by interleaving planning, querying, retrieval, integration, verification, and stopping. We analyze these systems through four architectural axes: retrieval coupling,"},{"source":"arxiv","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","authors":["Meng Ding","Mingxi Lei","Jie Zhang","Jinyan Liu","Di Wang"],"tags":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Evaluation","Alignment","RL for LLM"],"topics":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Evaluation","Alignment","RL for LLM"],"id":"arxiv:2610.30099","title":"Differentially Private Sparse Reward Estimation with Preference Feedback","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30099","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.30099.pdf","upvotes":0,"announceDate":"2026-10-22","date":"2026-10-22","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30099","abstract":"Reinforcement Learning with Human Feedback (RLHF) has become a central paradigm for aligning Large Language Models with human values. However, the reliance on sensitive preference data necessitates rigorous differential privacy (DP) guarantees to prevent user information leakage. Existing private alignment methods typically assume dense reward parameterizations, resulting in estimation error bounds that scale polynomially with the ambient {model size $d$}. Such dependence is prohibitive in modern {overparameterized models} where $d$ is vast. In this work, we study \\emph{Private Sparse RLHF} problem to address this limitation, assuming that human preferences (reward model) are governed by a subset of $s^* \\ll d$ relevant features. We provide the first comprehensive theoretical framework for differentially private sparse reward estimation from pairwise preference feedback under the Bradley"},{"source":"arxiv","submittedDate":"2026-10-20","authors":["Weizhe Xu","Jialiang Fan","Essien Taylor","Zijun Wang","Russell Joseph","Fanxin Kong"],"tags":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Planning","Alignment","Fine-tuning"],"topics":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Planning","Alignment","Fine-tuning"],"id":"arxiv:2610.28955","title":"SLoSa: Small, Low-Power, and Safe Foundation Models for Robotic Task Planning","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28955","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28955.pdf","upvotes":0,"announceDate":"2026-10-22","date":"2026-10-22","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28955","abstract":"Safe robotic task planning requires planners that can reliably achieve task goals while satisfying safety constraints. Classical planners search over explicit symbolic task representations and can provide formal guarantees with respect to encoded safety constraints, but require rigid input formats. Their computational cost also increases rapidly with the search space. In contrast, large language models (LLMs) offer flexible task interfaces and strong generalization capabilities, but they are large, power-hungry, and pre-trained with little robot-specific safety data and knowledge. Thus, they can be difficult to deploy locally on robotic systems with limited resources. To address these limitations, we present SLoSa, a neuro-symbolic compression and fine-tuning framework for training foundation-model-based robotic task planners with Small size, Low power demand, and Safety awareness. The f"},{"source":"arxiv","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","authors":["Zhennan Wan","Jianfei Chen"],"tags":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Reasoning","Evaluation","RL for LLM"],"topics":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Reasoning","Evaluation","RL for LLM"],"id":"arxiv:2610.30132","title":"LongZero: Enhancing Long-Context Reasoning in LLMs from Zero Data","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30132","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.30132.pdf","upvotes":0,"announceDate":"2026-10-22","date":"2026-10-22","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30132","abstract":"Reinforcement learning (RL) is an effective way to strengthen the long-context reasoning of large language models (LLMs), but it needs training data that are long, diverse, challenging, and verifiable. Human annotation of such data is expensive, naturally occurring data are too scarce to sustain RL, and existing synthesis methods depend on external corpora or stronger teacher models, which become harder to obtain as the trained models get stronger. Recently, RL from zero data, in which a model learns from tasks it proposes itself, has achieved excellent performance, but existing methods target short-context tasks. Therefore, we propose $\\textbf{LongZero}$, a method that lets an LLM improve its own long-context reasoning without any external data. Instead of writing questions and answers over existing documents, LongZero first has the model synthesize the question and answer with its supp"},{"source":"arxiv","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","authors":["Jielong Tang","Ling-An Zeng","Xujie Yuan","Xiao Dong","Zhiqian Xia","Qingfeng Li","Shixiong Kai","Kaichao Liang","Rong Yao","Jian Yin"],"tags":["Agentic Search","Reasoning","Memory","Evaluation","Multimodal Agent"],"topics":["Agentic Search","Reasoning","Memory","Evaluation","Multimodal Agent"],"id":"arxiv:2610.29995","title":"Remembering Through Change: Provenance-Preserving Hypergraph Memory for Multimodal Agents","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29995","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.29995.pdf","upvotes":0,"announceDate":"2026-10-22","date":"2026-10-22","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29995","abstract":"Long-term memory for multimodal agents remains challenging, as it must preserve fine-grained visual evidence while tracking entity evolution across sessions. Existing abstraction-based memories compress images into text, often discarding visually discriminative details, whereas pixel-preserving memories retain raw observations but struggle to reliably associate diverse observations with persistent entity identities and track their evolving states. To address these limitations, we propose MHG-Mem, a multimodal hypergraph memory that jointly organizes entity identity, state evolution, and provenance to visual evidence. Specifically, MHG-Mem represents distinct entity mentions and their observed facts as individual nodes, grouping identity-consistent mentions and facts through occurrence hyperedges. It then synthesizes aspect-specific evolution states from these grouped observations and lin"},{"source":"arxiv","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","authors":["Zixuan Yu","Zhenheng Tang","Xiaowen Chu","Bo Han"],"tags":["Agentic Search","Agent Safety","Error Attribution","Reasoning","Evaluation"],"topics":["Agentic Search","Agent Safety","Error Attribution","Reasoning","Evaluation"],"id":"arxiv:2610.29346","title":"Benchmarking and Error Analysis of Deep Search and Deep Research LLM Agents","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29346","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.29346.pdf","upvotes":0,"announceDate":"2026-10-22","date":"2026-10-22","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29346","abstract":"As Large Language Models (LLMs) evolve into autonomous Deep Search and Deep Research Agents capable of executing long-horizon, multi-step tasks, evaluating their reliability becomes increasingly critical. However, existing evaluation paradigms predominantly focus on final task outcomes, leaving a significant gap in understanding the intermediate execution traces, where agents are highly susceptible to various errors. To address this critical gap, we introduce a comprehensive methodology and analytical framework designed specifically to diagnose errors in intermediate workflows. Building upon this framework, we establish a structured taxonomy that categorizes these vulnerabilities into two primary dimensions: System-Level Errors and Task-Level Errors. Through a systematic examination of step-by-step execution traces and agent-environment interactions, we reveal how these underlying errors"},{"source":"arxiv","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","authors":["Jingzhe Zhang","Peixuan Zhang","Genying Li","Bing Ma","Shuchen Weng","Si Li","Boxin Shi"],"tags":["Post-Training","VLM Training","Reasoning","Planning","Reflection"],"topics":["Post-Training","VLM Training","Reasoning","Planning","Reflection"],"id":"arxiv:2610.29590","title":"CineEvolve: An Agentic Self-Evolution Framework for Cinematic Multi-Shot Video Generation","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29590","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.29590.pdf","upvotes":0,"announceDate":"2026-10-22","date":"2026-10-22","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29590","abstract":"Cinematic multi-shot video generation requires both narrative planning and iterative refinement. Existing methods either lack narrative reasoning or rely on feed-forward workflows without policy updates. In this paper, we propose CineEvolve, an agentic self-evolution framework for cinematic multi-shot video generation that unifies a Director and a Critic within a single vision-language policy. Emulating real-world filmmaking that couples iterative direction with critical feedback, we train CineEvolve via alternating reinforcement learning, where the Director surfaces flaws to sharpen the Critic, and the updated Critic guides the Director's targeted revisions. To stabilize co-evolution, we introduce Evolutionary GRPO (E-GRPO), leveraging historical anchoring and revision safeguarding to mitigate advantage inflation. Additionally, we contribute CineTraj, a 40K cinematic trajectory dataset "},{"source":"arxiv","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","authors":["Yufeng Li","Zaijing Li","Fuchen Zhang","Zongzong Wu","Ming Zhao","Fengxiao Tang"],"tags":["Agentic RAG","Agentic Search","Reasoning","Evaluation"],"topics":["Agentic RAG","Agentic Search","Reasoning","Evaluation"],"id":"arxiv:2610.29933","title":"Graph Topology Meets Energy-Driven Pathfinding: Preserving Contextual Consistency in Retrieval-Augmented Generation","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29933","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.29933.pdf","upvotes":0,"announceDate":"2026-10-22","date":"2026-10-22","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29933","abstract":"Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) frequently integrates knowledge graphs (KGs) for complex multi-hop question answering. However, their performance is increasingly bottlenecked by the knowledge structuring and reasoning processes. (i) Severe semantic loss: Distilling continuous texts into discrete symbols strips away contextual nuances and narrative coherence. (ii) Pronounced modality mismatch: Fragmented graph structures clash with the continuous texts LLMs are pre-trained on, while rewriting them into natural language introduces generative noise and omits information. To address these limitations, we propose ThreadRAG, a semantic-topology-based RAG framework for context-preserving knowledge structuring and modality-native evidence reasoning. ThreadRAG introduces a Semantic Topology Mapper (STM), which organizes retrieved documents into context-intact Semantic Nodes and constructs a "},{"source":"arxiv","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","authors":["Siddhesh Pawar","Chen Cecilia Liu","Anna Korhonen","Isabelle Augenstein"],"tags":["Post-Training","Evaluation","Alignment","Fine-tuning"],"topics":["Post-Training","Evaluation","Alignment","Fine-tuning"],"id":"arxiv:2610.29922","title":"Unintended Side Effects of Value Training in LLMs","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29922","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.29922.pdf","upvotes":0,"announceDate":"2026-10-22","date":"2026-10-22","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29922","abstract":"As LLMs are increasingly deployed in value-sensitive contexts, aligning them with human values has emerged as a post-training objective in NLP, aimed at instilling additional values and guiding model behaviour. However, current evaluations remain narrow. Because values are interconnected, it is unclear how fine-tuning for specific values alters unrelated capabilities and behaviours. In this work, we adapt four instruction-tuned LLMs across individual Hofstede's value dimensions and evaluate them on both cross-value transfer and broader behaviours. We find that value-specific training produces unintended side effects on untrained values, risk preferences, social cognition, and safety traits. Consequently, we suggest that value alignment after post-training should incorporate broader behavioral evaluations, and we highlight the need to investigate where additional value training might best"}],"archive":[],"latestPath":"data/latest.json"};
